module bindings.javascript.html_sanitizer_policy_table_widened_by_caller;

// An HTML sanitizer earns its keep by deciding inside the library which elements
// and attributes may be emitted, against a table its authors maintain from what
// browsers actually execute. Application code that reaches into that table and
// rewrites an entry before calling the sanitizer moves the decision back out to
// the caller, who is reasoning about one attribute at a time rather than about
// the parser the markup is eventually handed to -- and the rewrite outlives the
// call, because the table is the library's own module state.
//
// google-caja's html-css-sanitizer publishes exactly two such tables, and its
// documentation gives both a fixed vocabulary. `html4.ELEMENTS` maps a tag name
// to a bitmask of `html4.eflags`, of which `UNSAFE` marks the elements the
// sanitizer must never emit, so clearing that bit -- `html4.ELEMENTS.style &=
// ~html4.eflags.UNSAFE` -- puts the element back into the output. `html4.ATTRIBS`
// maps a `tag::attr` key to an `html4.atype`, where `URI` means "run this value
// through the URI policy" and `NONE`, spelled `0`, means "emit it unexamined";
// assigning `NONE` to a URL-bearing key such as `img::src` switches the
// sanitizer's scheme filtering off for that attribute, so a `src` naming a
// scheme the browser cannot load still reaches the document and the `onerror`
// handler beside it runs. Adding keys to `html4.ATTRIBS` from inside a tag
// policy is the same move made per document: the attribute names of the markup
// being sanitized choose which entries appear, so the input extends the
// allowlist that exists to constrain it.
//
// The fact recorded is the write itself, in either direction. A caller keeping
// its own copy of the sanitizer's policy owes a reviewer the argument for why
// the library's entry was wrong, and a relaxing write and the tightening write
// in the sibling branch are what have to be read together; recording only the
// relaxing half would report a policy that is toggled per call as if it were
// fixed. Reading the tables -- consulting `html4.ELEMENTS[tag]` to decide
// whether to log a removal -- is not a write and is not recorded.
//
// Function scope rather than module scope: the alias (`var ATTRIBS =
// caja.html4.ATTRIBS`) and the write through it are statements of one function,
// while a module-scope query over a bundled tree would be satisfied by any
// vendored copy of the sanitizer that merely defines the tables.
binding cajaSanitizerPolicyTableWidenedByCaller {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function"
    and node.context.language == "javascript"
    and containsAny(node.context.selector, ["html4.ATTRIBS", "html4.ELEMENTS"])
    and containsAny(node.context.assign, ["ATTRIBS", "ELEMENTS"])
  emit issue code.HtmlSanitizerLogicReview at node
}

// The same write reached through a local alias, which is how a tag policy that
// touches many keys is usually written (`var ATTRIBS = caja.html4.ATTRIBS;
// ATTRIBS[key] = 0`). The assignment's left side is then a subscript on a name
// the binding cannot predict, so the write is recognised by its shape --
// JavaScript's dynamic-property-write fact -- with the table still named by the
// alias's own initialiser in the same function. The negation keeps a function
// that already matched the dotted form above from being recorded twice.
binding cajaSanitizerPolicyTableWidenedThroughAlias {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function"
    and node.context.language == "javascript"
    and containsAny(node.context.selector, ["html4.ATTRIBS", "html4.ELEMENTS"])
    and node.context.dynamicPropertyWrite == "true"
    and not (containsAny(node.context.assign, ["ATTRIBS", "ELEMENTS"]))
  emit issue code.HtmlSanitizerLogicReview at node
}
